<Nav/>
<section class="legal-hero"><div class="in"><span class="badge">Seller Guide</span><h1>Selling the Traditional Way? Here's What Can Happen Before You Get Paid.</h1><p class="meta">Selling can involve a lot more than putting a sign in the yard.</p></div></section>
<main class="legal-wrap"><article class="legal-card article">
<p class="eyebrow"><a href="/blog/">&larr; More Seller Resources</a></p>
<div class="article-body">

<p style="font-size:20px;line-height:1.6;"><strong>Listing a property is not always as simple as putting it on the market and waiting for a check.</strong></p>
<p>Before you actually get paid, there can be preparation, showings, negotiations, inspections, price changes, buyer financing, appraisal, and closing steps in between.</p>

<div class="callout"><p><b>THE LISTING IS ONLY THE BEGINNING.</b><br/>Here is what the traditional process can look like before money reaches the seller.</p></div>

<div class="steps">
<div class="step"><div class="step-num">1</div><div><h2>Get the property ready</h2><p>Depending on the property, that can mean cleaning, decluttering, repairs, updates, landscaping, removing belongings, staging, and getting everything ready for photos and buyers.</p></div></div>

<div class="step"><div class="step-num">2</div><div><h2>Put it on the market</h2><p>The property gets photographed and marketed. Then come buyer appointments, showings, open houses where applicable, questions, feedback, and waiting for an offer you are willing to accept.</p></div></div>

<div class="step"><div class="step-num">3</div><div><h2>Possibly reduce the price</h2><p>If the property is not getting the response you expected, you may decide to reduce the asking price. Time on market, competing properties, condition, buyer feedback, and your own timeline can all affect that decision.</p></div></div>

<div class="step"><div class="step-num">4</div><div><h2>Accept an offer — but you're not done yet</h2><p>An accepted offer is an important step, but the transaction still has to make it through the requirements in the contract before you actually get paid.</p></div></div>

<div class="step"><div class="step-num">5</div><div><h2>Buyer inspections</h2><p>A traditional buyer may inspect the property. Depending on the contract, that can lead to requests for repairs, credits, concessions, or additional negotiating.</p></div></div>

<div class="step"><div class="step-num">6</div><div><h2>Appraisal and buyer financing</h2><p>If the buyer is using a mortgage, the lender process can include underwriting, approval, appraisal, and other requirements before the buyer is ready to close.</p></div></div>

<div class="step"><div class="step-num">7</div><div><h2>Keep paying for the property while you wait</h2><p>Until the property closes, expenses such as taxes, insurance, mortgage payments or interest, utilities, HOA charges, lawn care, maintenance, and repairs may continue where applicable.</p></div></div>

<div class="step"><div class="step-num">8</div><div><h2>Get to closing</h2><p>At closing, the costs and negotiated amounts that apply to the transaction can affect what the seller actually receives. Depending on the deal, that can include agent compensation, seller closing expenses, buyer concessions, credits, taxes, liens, mortgage payoff, and other property-specific obligations.</p></div></div>
</div>

<h2>And what if the first deal doesn't make it to closing?</h2>
<p>If a buyer cannot complete the transaction under the contract, the property may need to go back on the market. That can mean more time, more showings, more carrying costs, and another round of negotiations.</p>

